arXiv:2510.16591v1 [cs.LG] 18 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line" lang="en">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Cassidy Ashworth
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:cga28@cam.ac.uk">cga28@cam.ac.uk</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Cambridge, Cambridge, UK
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Pietro Liò
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:p1219@cam.ac.uk">p1219@cam.ac.uk</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Cambridge, Cambridge, UK
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Francesco Caso
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:francesco.caso@uniroma1.it">francesco.caso@uniroma1.it</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Cambridge, Cambridge, UK
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Rome, La Sapienza, Rome, Italy
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Deep learning models have proven enormously successful at using multiple layers of representation to learn relevant features of structured data. Encoding physical symmetries into these models can improve performance on difficult tasks, and recent work has motivated the principle of parameter symmetry breaking and restoration as a unifying mechanism underlying their hierarchical learning dynamics. We evaluate the role of parameter symmetry and network expressivity in the generalisation behaviour of neural networks when learning a real-space renormalisation group (RG) transformation, using the central limit theorem (CLT) as a test case map. We consider simple multilayer perceptrons (MLPs) and graph neural networks (GNNs), and vary weight symmetries and activation functions across architectures. Our results reveal a competition between symmetry constraints and expressivity, with overly complex or overconstrained models generalising poorly. We analytically demonstrate this poor generalisation behaviour for certain constrained MLP architectures by recasting the CLT as a cumulant recursion relation and making use of an established framework to propagate cumulants through MLPs. We also empirically validate an extension of this framework from MLPs to GNNs, elucidating the internal information processing performed by these more complex models. These findings offer new insight into the learning dynamics of symmetric networks and their limitations in modelling structured physical transformations.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The renormalisation group (RG) is a cornerstone of modern theoretical physics, used in areas ranging from critical phenomena in phase transitions to the Standard Model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. The RG method often proceeds à la Wilson, progressively eliminating high frequency modes to obtain large-scale system properties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. However, from a probabilistic viewpoint the RG can be interpreted as transforming distributions over physical degrees of freedom <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Order parameters are interpreted as random fields and the relevant limit distributions are reproduced upon coarse-graining, providing a clear interpretation of statistical universality. This focus on distributional transformations establishes a clear conceptual link between RG and neural networks, which can similarly be characterised as a mapping between input and output data distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. This link has been the subject of a number of recent works in which formal connections have been drawn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Neural networks have proven incredibly successful at solving a wide range of tasks, from molecule prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and material science <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> to modelling physical interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and improving PDE solvers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. While there has been considerable progress in theoretical understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, their precise decision-making mechanisms remain mostly opaque <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. A better understanding of the functional principles that underlie their success would allow networks to be more easily tailored to specific tasks. A promising avenue for improved interpretability and generalisation is the principled encoding of physical symmetries into network architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, with the added reciprocal benefit that better deep learning models can offer useful physical insight <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While prior work has focused on applying RG theory to directly improve model performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, here we shift the focus to instead improving an understanding of <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how</span> neural networks learn. We evaluate the role that parameter symmetry and network expressivity play in the generalisation of neural models learning physical transformations, using the RG-inspired central limit theorem (CLT) as a test case map. We empirically find a tension between symmetry constraints and network expressivity, and obtain analytical results to this end for certain simple Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) by recasting the CLT as a transformation of cumulants and theoretically propagating these cumulants through MLPs. We extend this propagation framework to graph neural networks (GNNs) and empirically validate our findings, improving the interpretability of these models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span>Theory</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">II.1 </span>CLT as an RG Transformation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following Jona-Lasinio <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, the CLT asserts the following. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1},\xi_{2},\ldots,\xi_{n},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1},\xi_{2},\ldots,\xi_{n},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of independent identically distributed (i.i.d.) random variables with finite variance <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=\mathbb{E}(\xi_{i}-\mathbb{E}(\xi_{i}))^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=\mathbb{E}(\xi_{i}-\mathbb{E}(\xi_{i}))^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}</annotation></semantics></math> indicating an expectation with respect to their common distribution. Then</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\sum_{i}(\xi_{i}-\mathbb{E}(\xi_{i}))}{\sigma\sqrt{n}}\xrightarrow{n\to\infty}N(0,1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\sum_{i}(\xi_{i}-\mathbb{E}(\xi_{i}))}{\sigma\sqrt{n}}\xrightarrow{n\to\infty}N(0,1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> the normal centered distribution of variance 1.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The system can be viewed as a real space decimation transformation on a one-dimensional Ising model. Consider <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as spins on a one-dimensional lattice <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and define <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\zeta_{n}^{1}=2^{-n/2}\sum_{i=1}^{2^{n}}\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></msup><mi>ξ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{n}^{1}=2^{-n/2}\sum_{i=1}^{2^{n}}\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\zeta_{n}^{2}=2^{-n/2}\sum_{i=2^{n}+1}^{2^{n+1}}\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msup><mi>ξ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{n}^{2}=2^{-n/2}\sum_{i=2^{n}+1}^{2^{n+1}}\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{n+1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\zeta_{n}^{1}+\zeta_{n}^{2}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{n+1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\zeta_{n}^{1}+\zeta_{n}^{2}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.E2" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></span>)</a> is the RG transformation that networks learn in subsequent generalisation experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The recursion relation for the corresponding distributions is</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{n+1}(x)=\sqrt{2}\int dy\,p_{n}(\sqrt{2}x-y)\,p_{n}(y)=(\mathcal{R}p_{n})(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{n+1}(x)=\sqrt{2}\int dy\,p_{n}(\sqrt{2}x-y)\,p_{n}(y)=(\mathcal{R}p_{n})(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">By considering the cumulant generating function, we recast <a href="#S2.E3" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></span>)</a> in terms of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>th order cumulants at RG step <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}^{(n+1)}=2^{1-r/2}\kappa_{r}^{(n)}\quad\text{for }r\geq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}^{(n+1)}=2^{1-r/2}\kappa_{r}^{(n)}\quad\text{for }r\geq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a> makes clear that all cumulants with <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math> are suppressed under the CLT. This result is used in <a href="#S2.SS4" title="II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.4</span></span></a> to obtain analytical solutions for symmetrically-constrained network weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By substitution it can be checked that the family of Gaussians</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{G,\sigma}(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^{2}}}e^{-x^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{G,\sigma}(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^{2}}}e^{-x^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">are fixed points of <a href="#S2.E3" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></span>)</a>, preserving normalisation, mean, and variance, for centred distributions. More detail is contained in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Central Limit Theorem Cumulant Recursion Relation ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">II.2 </span>Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs)</h3>

<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">II.2.1 </span>Network Architecture</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we outline the structure of MLPs, fully connected neural networks with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> neurons in each layer <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Layers consist of an affine transformation</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}^{l}=\sum_{j=1}^{N_{l-1}}\,W_{ij}^{l}\,y_{j}^{l-1}+b_{i}^{l}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}^{l}=\sum_{j=1}^{N_{l-1}}\,W_{ij}^{l}\,y_{j}^{l-1}+b_{i}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">parameterized by a weight matrix <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{l}\times N_{l-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{l}\times N_{l-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and bias vector <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A nonlinear activation function <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is then applied elementwise such that the full layer is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}^{l}=\phi\big(z_{i}^{l}\big)=\phi\left(\sum_{j=1}^{N_{l-1}}\,W_{ij}^{l}\,y_{j}^{l-1}+b_{i}^{l}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}^{l}=\phi\big(z_{i}^{l}\big)=\phi\left(\sum_{j=1}^{N_{l-1}}\,W_{ij}^{l}\,y_{j}^{l-1}+b_{i}^{l}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Input data of dimension <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{0}=x\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{0}=x\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Iterating over layers defines the network mapping <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=g(x;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=g(x;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> for parameters <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta:=\{W^{l},b^{l}\}_{l=1,\dots,L+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta:=\{W^{l},b^{l}\}_{l=1,\dots,L+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the output <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=z_{i}^{L+1},\;y\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=z_{i}^{L+1},\;y\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by a final linear readout layer.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.16591v1/visual_abstract_portrait_cbgsf.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/483;" width="476" height="483" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>(a) Network analysis based on data samples considers a direct mapping between samples <math id="S2.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and outputs <math id="S2.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> comprised of affine and nonlinear transformations. (b) The alternative statistical viewpoint considers how networks transform the entire data distribution <math id="S2.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Affine layers propagate cumulants independently but the nonlinearity mixes cumulants of different orders <math id="S2.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. A Gaussian input with only non-zero mean <math id="S2.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and covariance <math id="S2.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is shown. Figure taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">II.2.2 </span>Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following Fischer et al <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, here we outline how cumulants propagate through MLP networks. For more detail, see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Cumulants are the preferred distributional parameterisation as they are additive under addition of independent variables, leading to simpler expressions for their propagation. Given the network mapping <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:x\mapsto y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:x\mapsto y</annotation></semantics></math> we could relate the cumulant generating function of the outputs <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> to that of the inputs <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{y|\theta}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{y|\theta}(j)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}y)\rangle_{y|\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}y)\rangle_{y|\theta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}g(x;\theta))\rangle_{x}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}g(x;\theta))\rangle_{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The output cumulant of order <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the derivative</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{y|\theta}^{(n)}=\left.\frac{d^{n}\mathcal{W}_{y|\theta}(j)}{dj^{n}}\right|_{j=0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{y|\theta}^{(n)}=\left.\frac{d^{n}\mathcal{W}_{y|\theta}(j)}{dj^{n}}\right|_{j=0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">In principle, evaluating <a href="#S2.E9" title="In II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></span>)</a> would yield a direct relation between input and output cumulants. However, while the nonlinear activation <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is critical for network expressivity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, it makes direct evaluation of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{y|\theta}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{y|\theta}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> difficult. Instead, we focus on tracking the layerwise cumulant propagation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An affine linear transformation of the form <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=W_{ij}x_{j}+b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=W_{ij}x_{j}+b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> relates the preactivations <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> to the postactivations of the previous layer <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding cumulant generating function transformation is</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{z^{l}}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{z^{l}}(j)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}z^{l})\rangle_{z^{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}z^{l})\rangle_{z^{l}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}W^{l}y^{l-1}+j^{\top}b^{l})\rangle_{y^{l-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}W^{l}y^{l-1}+j^{\top}b^{l})\rangle_{y^{l-1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{W}_{y^{l-1}}((W^{l})^{\top}j)+j^{\top}b^{l}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{W}_{y^{l-1}}((W^{l})^{\top}j)+j^{\top}b^{l},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which gives</p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{z^{l}}^{(1)}=W^{l}G_{y^{l-1}}^{(1)}+b^{l}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{z^{l}}^{(1)}=W^{l}G_{y^{l-1}}^{(1)}+b^{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">for the first order cumulant (<math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>), and</p>
<table id="S2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="G_{z^{l},i_{1},\dots,i_{n}}^{(n)}=\sum_{s_{1},\dots,s_{n}}W^{l}_{i_{1}s_{1}}\cdots W^{l}_{i_{n}s_{n}}\,G_{y^{l-1},s_{1},\dots,s_{n}}^{(n)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{z^{l},i_{1},\dots,i_{n}}^{(n)}=\sum_{s_{1},\dots,s_{n}}W^{l}_{i_{1}s_{1}}\cdots W^{l}_{i_{n}s_{n}}\,G_{y^{l-1},s_{1},\dots,s_{n}}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We can write the equivalent transformation across the nonlinear activation function <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> within each layer <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{y^{l}}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{y^{l}}(j)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}y^{l})\rangle_{y^{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}y^{l})\rangle_{y^{l}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}\phi(z^{l}))\rangle_{z^{l}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}\phi(z^{l}))\rangle_{z^{l}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In general, <a href="#S2.E14" title="In II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></span>)</a> cannot be evaluated exactly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. However, perturbative techniques exist to reconstruct postactivation cumulants as functions of preactivation equivalents, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{y^{l}}^{(n)}=f_{n}(\{G_{z^{l}}^{(m)}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{y^{l}}^{(n)}=f_{n}(\{G_{z^{l}}^{(m)}\})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. This mixing of cumulants represents the information transfer between layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.E12" title="In II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></span>)</a>, <a href="#S2.E13" title="In II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></span>)</a>, and the relevant interaction functions <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{n}(\{G_{z^{l}}^{(m)}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{n}(\{G_{z^{l}}^{(m)}\})</annotation></semantics></math> can be composed to define a <span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">statistical model</span> corresponding any given network as</p>
<table id="S2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{stat}}:\left(\left\{G_{x}^{(m)}\right\}_{m},\theta,\phi\right)\mapsto p(y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>stat</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{stat}}:\left(\left\{G_{x}^{(m)}\right\}_{m},\theta,\phi\right)\mapsto p(y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is characterised by the propagated cumulants</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{y}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{y}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=W^{L+1}\left(f_{n}\left(\cdots\{G_{x}^{(m)}\}_{m}\cdots\right)\right)+b^{L+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=W^{L+1}\left(f_{n}\left(\cdots\{G_{x}^{(m)}\}_{m}\cdots\right)\right)+b^{L+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=:g_{n}\left(\{G_{x}^{(m)}\}_{m};\theta,\phi\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=:g_{n}\left(\{G_{x}^{(m)}\}_{m};\theta,\phi\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">This framework characterises MLPs as mappings of data distributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">II.3 </span>Graph Neural Networks (GNNs)</h3>

<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">II.3.1 </span>Network Architecture</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can define a general graph <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V,E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V,E})</annotation></semantics></math> with nodes <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, edges <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{ij}\in\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{ij}\in\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional node feature vectors <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, and denote the set of nodes neighbouring <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(i)</annotation></semantics></math>. GNNs use a message passing framework whereby each <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is iteratively updated using information aggregated across its neighbourhood <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. The different iterations are known as the layers of a GNN. The basic update can be expressed as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{u}^{(k+1)}=\psi\left(h_{u}^{(k)},m_{\mathcal{N}(u)}^{(k)}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{u}^{(k+1)}=\psi\left(h_{u}^{(k)},m_{\mathcal{N}(u)}^{(k)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is usually an MLP, and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathcal{N}(u)}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathcal{N}(u)}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the aggregated message</p>
<table id="S2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m_{\mathcal{N}(u)}^{(k)}=\bigoplus_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}\left\{h_{v}^{(k)},\forall v\in\mathcal{N}(u)\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathcal{N}(u)}^{(k)}=\bigoplus_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}\left\{h_{v}^{(k)},\forall v\in\mathcal{N}(u)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for aggregation <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which must be permutation invariant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Set pooling operations like sum (<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}</annotation></semantics></math>) or mean (<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{1}{N}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{N}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}</annotation></semantics></math>) are commonly used.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/GNN_aggregation.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/145;" width="476" height="145"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of how a single node aggregates messages for a two-layer model. The GNN computation graph forms a tree structure as messages are sequentially aggregated from each node’s local neighbourhoods. Figure taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Depending on the type of GNN, multiple learnable MLPs can be included in the framework to increase expressivity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. However, we consider a simple architecture to simplify cumulant propagation. SAGEConv layers are implemented using <span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PyTorch Geometric</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> with sum aggregation and a single linear transformation, and no biases or nonlinear activation functions. These layers follow the update rule</p>
<table id="S2.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{u}^{(k+1)}=W_{self}^{(k+1)}h_{u}^{(k)}+W_{neigh}^{(k+1)}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}h_{v}^{(k)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{u}^{(k+1)}=W_{self}^{(k+1)}h_{u}^{(k)}+W_{neigh}^{(k+1)}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}h_{v}^{(k)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Given input data <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> we can iteratively apply this to obtain output node embeddings <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">II.3.2 </span>Decomposing GNNs as Mappings of Correlation Functions</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we extend the cumulant propagation framework in <a href="#S2.SS2.SSS2" title="II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.2.2</span></a> to GNNs. Similarly to MLPs, we consider layerwise cumulant propagation, and consider a generalised version of <a href="#S2.E19" title="In II.3.1 Network Architecture ‣ II.3 Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></span>)</a> with biases:</p>
<table id="S2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{u}^{k}=W_{self}^{k}h_{u}^{k-1}+W_{neigh}^{k}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}h_{v}^{k-1}+b^{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{u}^{k}=W_{self}^{k}h_{u}^{k-1}+W_{neigh}^{k}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}h_{v}^{k-1}+b^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Following <a href="#S2.E11" title="In II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></span>)</a> we can write the cumulant generating function transformation as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{z_{u}^{k}}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{z_{u}^{k}}(j)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}z_{u}^{k})\rangle_{z_{u}^{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}z_{u}^{k})\rangle_{z_{u}^{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\ln\Big\langle\exp\Big[j^{\top}W_{\text{self}}^{k}h_{u}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ln</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>self</mtext><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ln\Big\langle\exp\Big[j^{\top}W_{\text{self}}^{k}h_{u}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+j^{\top}W_{\text{neigh}}^{k}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}h_{v}^{k-1}+j^{\top}b^{k}\Big]\Big\rangle_{h_{u,v}^{k-1}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>neigh</mtext><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+j^{\top}W_{\text{neigh}}^{k}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}h_{v}^{k-1}+j^{\top}b^{k}\Big]\Big\rangle_{h_{u,v}^{k-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">As the expectation is taken with respect to the joint distribution over all nodes within the neighbourhood of node <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, this expression is difficult to evaluate. Guided by the CLT that uses i.i.d. variables, at a first approximation we can assume nodes are independent and write</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{z_{u}^{k}}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{z_{u}^{k}}(j)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{W}_{h_{u}^{k-1}}((W_{self}^{k})^{\top}j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{W}_{h_{u}^{k-1}}((W_{self}^{k})^{\top}j)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}\mathcal{W}_{h_{v}^{k-1}}((W_{neigh}^{k})^{\top}j)+j^{\top}b^{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}\mathcal{W}_{h_{v}^{k-1}}((W_{neigh}^{k})^{\top}j)+j^{\top}b^{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">yielding for the first order cumulant (<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>)</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{z_{u}^{k}}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{z_{u}^{k}}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=W_{self}^{k}G_{h_{u}^{k-1}}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=W_{self}^{k}G_{h_{u}^{k-1}}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}W_{neigh}^{k}G_{h_{v}^{k-1}}^{(1)}+b^{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\sum_{v\in\mathcal{N}(u)}W_{neigh}^{k}G_{h_{v}^{k-1}}^{(1)}+b^{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">and for <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{z_{u}^{k},i_{1},\dots,i_{n}}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{z_{u}^{k},i_{1},\dots,i_{n}}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{s_{1},\dots,s_{n}}W_{self,\;i_{1}s_{1}}^{k}\cdots W^{k}_{self,\;i_{n}s_{n}}\,G_{h_{u}^{k-1},s_{1},\dots,s_{n}}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{s_{1},\dots,s_{n}}W_{self,\;i_{1}s_{1}}^{k}\cdots W^{k}_{self,\;i_{n}s_{n}}\,G_{h_{u}^{k-1},s_{1},\dots,s_{n}}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip-25.00003pt+\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}v\in\mathcal{N}(u)\\
s_{1},\dots,s_{n}\end{subarray}}W_{i_{1}s_{1}}^{k,neigh}\cdots W_{i_{n}s_{n}}^{k,neigh}G_{h_{v}^{k-1},s_{1},\dots,s_{n}}^{(n)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip-25.00003pt+\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}v\in\mathcal{N}(u)\\
s_{1},\dots,s_{n}\end{subarray}}W_{i_{1}s_{1}}^{k,neigh}\cdots W_{i_{n}s_{n}}^{k,neigh}G_{h_{v}^{k-1},s_{1},\dots,s_{n}}^{(n)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">If a nonlinear activation function <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(z_{u}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(z_{u}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> were used, perturbative techniques could be used to determine the interactions between cumulants of different orders <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. However, for simplicity we only consider this simple case of affine linear transformations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">II.4 </span>CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Integrating inductive priors into network design can improve network training and generalisation to unseen tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. This section develops analytical solutions for symmetrically-constrained weights in simple MLP networks that implement the CLT transformation (<a href="#S2.SS1" title="II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.1</span></span></a>); their empirical generalisation is investigated in later sections.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">II.4.1 </span>Linear Networks</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a> shows how cumulants evolve under the CLT. We can therefore find equations relating input and output cumulants by using the propagation framework in <a href="#S2.SS2.SSS2" title="II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.2.2</span></a>. These equations can be inverted to solve for corresponding network weights. Results are given here, but for more detail see <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a> is interpreted as a decimation transformation from two to one dimensions, and it is clear that the transformation exhibits an <math id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> symmetry corresponding to exchange of the variables <math id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{n}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{n}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. To ‘truly’ learn this function, we expect learned network mappings should be invariant under the same symmetry, leading to constraints on the weights. In the simplest case of a network with a single linear layer and no biases, the weights are easily found to be</p>
<table id="S2.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=\begin{pmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}&amp;\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=\begin{pmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}&amp;\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the case of networks with no biases and a single two-dimensional hidden layer, we have the network mapping</p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{i}=g(x_{i};\theta)=W_{ij}^{2}W_{jk}^{1}x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{i}=g(x_{i};\theta)=W_{ij}^{2}W_{jk}^{1}x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(\xi_{n}^{1},\xi_{n}^{2})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(\xi_{n}^{1},\xi_{n}^{2})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. We seek <math id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)=g(Px)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)=g(Px)</annotation></semantics></math> for permutation matrix</p>
<table id="S2.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\
1&amp;0\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\
1&amp;0\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">leading to the constraint</p>
<table id="S2.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{2}P^{\top}W^{1}P=W^{2}W^{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{2}P^{\top}W^{1}P=W^{2}W^{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Solving this for <math id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S2.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{0}&amp;w_{1}\\
w_{1}&amp;w_{0}\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{0}&amp;w_{1}\\
w_{1}&amp;w_{0}\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{2}&amp;w_{2}\end{pmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{2}&amp;w_{2}\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p3.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has been chosen to treat both hidden units equally.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The CLT considers i.i.d. variables so we expect diagonal input cumulants, for example</p>
<table id="S2.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{x}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{x}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}\kappa_{1}\\
\kappa_{1}\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}\kappa_{1}\\
\kappa_{1}\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{x}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{x}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}\kappa_{2}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\kappa_{2}\end{pmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}\kappa_{2}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\kappa_{2}\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a> shows that cumulants obey a simple scaling law with <math id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{y}^{(1)}=\sqrt{2}G_{x}^{(1)},G_{y}^{(2)}=G_{x}^{(2)},G_{y}^{(3)}=1/\sqrt{2}G_{x}^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{y}^{(1)}=\sqrt{2}G_{x}^{(1)},G_{y}^{(2)}=G_{x}^{(2)},G_{y}^{(3)}=1/\sqrt{2}G_{x}^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so on. Transforming these first three cumulants according to <a href="#S2.E12" title="In II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></span>)</a> and <a href="#S2.E13" title="In II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></span>)</a> yields 3 conditions, which is enough to fully determine the weights in <a href="#S2.E29" title="In II.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></span>)</a> and <a href="#S2.E30" title="In II.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></span>)</a>. They form a surface in parameter space that satisfies</p>
<table id="S2.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}=\frac{1}{w_{2}\sqrt{2}}-w_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}=\frac{1}{w_{2}\sqrt{2}}-w_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We can introduce biases into these two-dimensional networks by similarly choosing them to treat both inputs equally; for the first layer we have <math id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=(b_{1},b_{1})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=(b_{1},b_{1})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, and as the output is one-dimensional we have <math id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> only for the second layer. Extra parameters required more conditions from higher order cumulants. We again find that weights all lie in a subspace given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{w_{2}\sqrt{2}}-w_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{w_{2}\sqrt{2}}-w_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-2w_{2}b_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-2w_{2}b_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">II.4.2 </span>Quadratic Nonlinearity</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can use results given in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></span></a> for cumulant propagation across a quadratic nonlinearity <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(z)=z+\alpha z^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(z)=z+\alpha z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to solve for network weights as above. Using the same network architecture, with no biases and the weights in <a href="#S2.E29" title="In II.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></span>)</a> and <a href="#S2.E30" title="In II.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></span>)</a>, we find that the first cumulant transforms as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\kappa_{1}\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\kappa_{1}\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=2w_{2}\Big[(w_{0}+w_{1})\kappa_{1}+\alpha\kappa_{1}^{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2w_{2}\Big[(w_{0}+w_{1})\kappa_{1}+\alpha\kappa_{1}^{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\alpha\kappa_{2}(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})\Big]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\alpha\kappa_{2}(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})\Big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The nonlinearity mixes cumulants of different orders, yet <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a> suggests a clean scaling of each <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>. As this expression should be true for any cumulants <math id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, by comparing coefficients we find that</p>
<table id="S2.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">These equations have an inconsistency, suggesting that networks with a quadratic nonlinearity and symmetric weights cannot truly learn the transformation in <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a>, and any learnt solution must be a poor approximation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span>Generalisation of MLPs</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">III.1 </span>Tasks</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In keeping with the probabilistic interpretation of the RG, we probe the generalisation of MLPs via two distinct tasks designed to test different distributional shifts. In both cases, the quality of learned transformations was assessed by networks’ ability to successfully capture distributional structure of unseen i.i.d. inputs (see <a href="#S3.SS3" title="III.3 Evaluation Metrics ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.3</span></span></a>), which are all expected to tend to Gaussians under the CLT.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gaussian task</span> considers input Gaussians with varying variance over a single RG step (i.e., one iteration of the network), evaluating generalisation under shifts that preserve low-order cumulants, e.g. mean and variance. In contrast, the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uniform task</span> evolves off-centre uniform distributions on the interval <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> over multiple composed RG iterations, introducing non-zero higher-order cumulants with more complex behaviour. Both tasks were used to study the effect of symmetry and nonlinearity within the network by varying activation functions and weight constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">III.2 </span>Network Architecture and Training Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To directly investigate the effect of symmetry constraints discussed in <a href="#S2.SS4" title="II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.4</span></span></a>, we used shallow networks with fixed width <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{l}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{l}=2</annotation></semantics></math> and a single <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1</annotation></semantics></math> hidden layer that follow the architecture defined in <a href="#S2.SS2.SSS1" title="II.2.1 Network Architecture ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.2.1</span></a>. Networks were trained under explicit weight constraints as in <a href="#S2.E29" title="In II.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></span>)</a> and <a href="#S2.E30" title="In II.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></span>)</a>, and different activation functions were used to systematically vary both the degree of nonlinearity and extent of asymmetry of the activation function itself. For example, the quadratic activation <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(z)=z+\alpha z^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(z)=z+\alpha z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is minimally nonlinear yet preserves symmetry, while ReLU is both strongly nonlinear and asymmetric. This dual variation allows disentanglement of the impact of architectural and functional symmetry on generalisation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gaussian task</span> we tested purely linear networks, minimally nonlinear networks using the quadratic activation <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(z)=z+\alpha z^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(z)=z+\alpha z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, and strongly nonlinear networks with the ReLU activation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. The <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uniform task</span> additionally considered leaky ReLU activations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> with gradients in the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> region ranging from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (effectively a linear network with no activation) to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (standard ReLU), to interpolate between these extremal cases. To investigate how more expressive learnable functions might improve generalisation, a spline nonlinearity with trainable parameters was also considered both with and without frozen weights. Catmull-Rom-like cubic b-splines were used <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> with a smoothness regularisation term in the loss to discourage overfitting, and the interpolation range set adaptively for each network initialisation by considering the range of the spline inputs after the first affine layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All synthetic training and test data sets consisted of i.i.d. sample pairs drawn from the relevant distributions, with ground truth values comprising of their decimation outputs. For the uniform task, shuffled duplicates of network outputs were used as input variables in consecutive RG to remove all data correlations and ensure i.i.d. behaviour. Due to numerical sampling fluctuations, for each task a disparity was observed between empirical ground truth cumulants and the average values of those predicted from analytically scaling input cumulants as per <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a>. Additionally, empirical input tensor cumulants were not exactly diagonal, as expected in e.g. <a href="#S2.E32" title="In II.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></span>)</a> due to finite sampling. A fixed data set size of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> was used to balance cumulant estimation accuracy and computational speed (see <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experimental Setup ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></span></a> for more). Networks were implemented using <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PyTorch</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, and code is available <a href="https://github.com/cass24687/Part-III-Project-Repo" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">here</a>. Further key hyperparameters and training details are contained in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experimental Setup ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">III.3 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While standard generalisation metrics such as classification accuracy or raw MSE are commonly used in supervised learning benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, in this context they are not appropriate. Rather than pointwise predictive performance, the focus is instead on evaluating the quality of learned transformations that preserve distributional structure. We therefore adopt empirical cumulant deviation and Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> as primary metrics to assess generalisation, which better quantify how well the transformed outputs preserve statistical alignment with the ground truth. Empirical cumulant deviation measures the difference between individual cumulants of the network output <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{net}}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{net}}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> and the ground truth distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{gt}}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{gt}}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a quantitative measure of the disagreement between two probability distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>; here, we use it to more holistically assess how closely <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{net}}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{net}}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> matches <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{gt}}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{gt}}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Following Fischer et al., to account for any variability of output distributions across different network realisations, we normalise <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> by the Shannon entropy <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> of the model output, yielding</p>
<table id="S3.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E37.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})=\frac{D_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})}{H(p_{\text{net}})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})=\frac{D_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})}{H(p_{\text{net}})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compute averaged empirical cumulants up to fourth order from multiple repeat experiments. The variance of sample cumulants of order <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> scales roughly as <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/N)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of repeats <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>; five different seeds were used to suitably reduce estimation noise. As tensor input cumulants are required for propagation (<a href="#S2.SS2.SSS2" title="II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.2.2</span></a>), when investigating this framework higher order cumulants were explicitly symmetrised to preserve the expected property of multilinear symmetry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. Off-diagonal elements were retained to reflect sampling imperfections in their corresponding input distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The discrete KL divergence was calculated between empirical histograms of network outputs and ground truth values. Where the cumulant values only were obtained,the KL divergence was determined with reconstructed probability densities using Fourier inversion of the characteristic function. Histogram bins were aligned to the reconstruction grid, and a frequency cutoff <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{max}}=80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{max}}=80</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{13}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>13</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{13}</annotation></semantics></math> frequency sampling points were suitable to prevent aliasing while capturing the full range of the characteristic function.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">III.4 </span>Gaussian Task Results</h3>

<section id="S3.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">III.4.1 </span>Linear Networks</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by evaluating the effect of symmetric weight constraints on the generalisation of linear networks. The normalised KL divergence between network output and ground truth distributions <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math> decreases inversely with input variance, suggesting networks improved generalisation at larger variances (<a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ III.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ III.4 Gaussian Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></span></a>). In all cases <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> remains very small, of order <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, with minimal variation between the two models. Additionally, the trained symmetric weights were found to satisfy the constraint equations <a href="#S2.E34" title="In II.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></span>)</a> to within a numerical tolerance of <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, validating the above analysis. These results establish a baseline for comparison with more expressive network architectures in later sections.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/final_linear_KL.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Normalised KL divergence <math id="S3.F3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of input distribution variance <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for linear networks. Error bars were negligible and are omitted.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/nonlinear_asymm_MAIN_wnumber.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/150;" width="476" height="150"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>(a) Normalised KL divergence <math id="S3.F4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of input variance <math id="S3.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. (b) Fractional deviations of second order cumulants <math id="S3.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> between network outputs and ground truth, expressed as percentages, as a function of variance <math id="S3.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The three conditions shown all use unconstrained asymmetric weights. Error bars were negligible in (a) and are omitted.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/nonlinear_symm_MAIN_wnumbers.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span> (a), (b) Normalised KL divergence <math id="S3.F5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of input variance <math id="S3.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. (c), (d) Relative deviations of second order cumulants <math id="S3.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, expressed as percentages, as a function of variance <math id="S3.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Left hand panels show all three activation types (linear, ReLU, quadratic); right hand panels show only linear networks and quadratic activations. All networks use symmetrically-constrained weights.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">III.4.2 </span>Nonlinear Networks with Asymmetric Weights</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next evaluate the impact of nonlinearity and asymmetry within the activation function on generalisation, using networks with unconstrained asymmetric weights. There is minimal variation in <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math> across linear, ReLU, and quadratic activations, with all models exhibiting improved generalisation at larger input variances (<a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ III.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ III.4 Gaussian Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span></a>). However, these similarities do not extend to the individual cumulants. While both linear networks and those with ReLU activation predict target <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> values well, networks with quadratic activation consistently overestimate <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by approximately 0.02% for all input variances. Although deviations are all small, these differences are systematic, highlighting the increased expressivity of stronger nonlinearities. Higher order cumulants (<math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>) vanish for Gaussian distributions so we present results for <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> only.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">III.4.3 </span>Nonlinear Networks with Symmetric Weights</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now consider the combined effect of applying symmetry constraints to both network weights and activation functions. The results in <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ III.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ III.4 Gaussian Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></span></a> show a marked difference in behaviour from the unconstrained case in <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ III.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ III.4 Gaussian Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span></a>. <math id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> for the ReLU activation exhibits a dramatic increase, being approximately four orders of magnitude greater, and for the quadratic activation <math id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> similarly increases by a factor of ten. These discrepancies are reflected in the <math id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> deviations. The ReLU activation displays a very high <math id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation that systematically exceeds 60%, while the deviation for the quadratic nonlinearity is two orders of magnitude greater than in the asymmetric case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The expected trend of <math id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation decreasing with variance is observed for linear and quadratic networks. In contrast, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> for both ReLU and quadratic activations increases with variance. This behaviour suggests poor matching of higher order cumulants, highlighting the brittleness of the learned transformations. These results are consistent with those of <a href="#S2.SS4.SSS2" title="II.4.2 Quadratic Nonlinearity ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.4.2</span></a>, where it is shown that no analytical solution exists for networks of this size with symmetric weights and quadratic nonlinearities. The findings here indicate that strict symmetry constraints can hinder network expressivity when symmetry breaking is required for learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">III.5 </span>Uniform Task Results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Linear networks exhibited similar generalisation performance as in the Gaussian task, with minimal difference between networks with symmetric and unconstrained weights and small <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> values. We therefore do not focus explicitly on their behaviour, instead using them as a benchmark for comparison where appropriate. Additionally, as the uniform task is designed to assess generalisation under transformations involving higher order cumulants, we present results for <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> only, with those for <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> provided in certain cases in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E MLP Uniform Task: Leaky ReLU Results ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">III.5.1 </span>Quadratic Activation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section continues the discussion of task-dependent symmetry breaking by focusing on networks with quadratic nonlinearity and examining the effects of symmetric and unconstrained weights on generalisation. The results in <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ III.5.2 Leaky ReLU with Asymmetric Weights ‣ III.5 Uniform Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></span></a> are concordant with both <a href="#S3.SS4.SSS3" title="III.4.3 Nonlinear Networks with Symmetric Weights ‣ III.4 Gaussian Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S2.SS4.SSS2" title="II.4.2 Quadratic Nonlinearity ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.4.2</span></a>: networks with unconstrained weights significantly outperform their symmetric counterparts, which quickly accumulate error over successive RG steps. In the symmetric case, <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> increases by up to four orders of magnitude compared to the linear baseline, while networks with asymmetric weights maintain values close to zero throughout. Similar behaviour is observed for the relative <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation, which not only diverges to approximately <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60\times 10^{3}\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60\times 10^{3}\%</annotation></semantics></math> in the symmetric case, but changes sign too, highlighting the breakdown in the learned transformation’s ability to preserve higher order distributional structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">III.5.2 </span>Leaky ReLU with Asymmetric Weights</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we consider networks with asymmetric weights and leaky ReLU activation, and smoothly vary the negative slope from <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (a linear network) to <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (corresponding to standard ReLU) to isolate the role that asymmetry and nonlinearity within the activation function plays in learning. <a href="#S3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ III.5.2 Leaky ReLU with Asymmetric Weights ‣ III.5 Uniform Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></span></a> shows that a gradient of <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits particularly poor generalisation. For this case, <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> reaches up to <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.04</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.04</annotation></semantics></math>; while significantly smaller than those observed for the quadratic nonlinearity in <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ III.5.2 Leaky ReLU with Asymmetric Weights ‣ III.5 Uniform Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></span></a>, values are still two orders of magnitude larger than the linear benchmark. There is little variation in <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> amongst the other gradients considered, though a consistent spike is observed across all conditions at the first RG step.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/FINAL_leaky_slopes.svg" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/316;" width="476" height="316"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Schematic showing the different slopes of leaky ReLU investigated.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/QUADRATIC_PANEL_FINAL_6.25.svg" id="S3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/143;" width="476" height="143"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span> (a) Normalised KL divergence <math id="S3.F7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and (b) relative <math id="S3.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation, both plotted as a function of the number of successive RG steps. Both panels show networks with constrained and unconstrained weights.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.16591v1/final_asymm_leakyrelu_main.png" id="S3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/287;" width="476" height="287" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span> (a), (b) Normalised KL divergence <math id="S3.F8.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and (c), (d) relative <math id="S3.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation, both as a function of the number of RG steps. Left and right hand panels are plotted with and without <math id="S3.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> gradient. All networks have unconstrained weights.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation plots reveal a more nuanced picture. Despite the fact that values are again substantially smaller than for the quadratic nonlinearity, there is a clear performance hierarchy. Networks with slope <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> perform comparably to purely linear equivalents, with deviations of approximately <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01\%</annotation></semantics></math>. However, slightly perturbing the gradient to <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> drastically reduces performance, which improves as the degree of nonlinearity is increased, until networks outperform the linear baseline. This behaviour resembles a symmetry-breaking transition, analogous to phase transitions in physical systems like ferromagnets, where a small change in some control parameter induces a sharp qualitative shift in the system. Similar trends are observed in <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and are included in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E MLP Uniform Task: Leaky ReLU Results ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">III.5.3 </span>Leaky ReLU with Symmetric Weights</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section the same analysis as <a href="#S3.SS5.SSS2" title="III.5.2 Leaky ReLU with Asymmetric Weights ‣ III.5 Uniform Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.5.2</span></a> is repeated but using symmetric weights. <a href="#S3.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ III.5.4 Spline Activation with Asymmetric Weights ‣ III.5 Uniform Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></span></a> shows that performance is generally worse across the different leaky ReLU gradients than in the previous asymmetric weights case. The <math id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> values are two orders of magnitude greater, with larger errors that suggest increased variability across network initialisations. This behaviour is reflected in <math id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation values, which are three orders of magnitude greater than the asymmetric case. Both metrics have large errors that stem from reduced training stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both the linear and ReLU conditions generalise the best, and exactly the same as each other, while the <math id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> gradient is clearly the worst. At these intermediate slopes, neither symmetric nor asymmetric structure dominates, reducing the network’s ability to generalise. This behaviour is reminiscent of frustrated regimes in physical systems, where competing symmetries or constraints prevent optimal ordering. Furthermore, the relative <math id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation plateaus after just two RG iterations and remains constant thereafter, while the <math id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> continues to grow. This difference highlights the architectural bottleneck: the network rapidly converges to a limited internal representation which causes higher order cumulants to decay at a rate that is offset by some fixed amount to the true transformation, and the full distributional mismatch increases. Results for <math id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are included in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E MLP Uniform Task: Leaky ReLU Results ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/reg_spline_frozenweights_unfrozenspline.svg" id="S3.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/298;" width="452" height="298"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Unstable spline shape when only spline parameters are trainable.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/spline_shape_all_unfrozen.svg" id="S3.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:1047/624;" width="1047" height="624"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Linear spline shape when both weights and spline parameters are learnable.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">III.5.4 </span>Spline Activation with Asymmetric Weights</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we consider networks with a learnable spline nonlinearity under two conditions of fixed or trainable weights. We did not impose symmetry constraints on weights in either case. Rather than directly investigating symmetry preservation of the transformation, the focus was instead on how generalisation is affected when models are allowed to share the learning load between the activation function and weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S3.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ III.5.4 Spline Activation with Asymmetric Weights ‣ III.5 Uniform Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></span></a>, the ‘Spline’ configuration with only learnable spline parameters did not generalise well. It quickly accumulated large errors, with <math id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> values increasing roughly tenfold over the total number of iterations and the relative <math id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation constant at approximately <math id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Expressivity is severely limited by fixed weights that vary randomly with initialisation, which the spline compensates for with unstable and overfitted spline shapes as in <a href="#S3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ III.5.3 Leaky ReLU with Symmetric Weights ‣ III.5 Uniform Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, when both the weights and spline parameters are trainable, networks learn a linear architecture with spline shapes remaining linear as in <a href="#S3.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ III.5.3 Leaky ReLU with Symmetric Weights ‣ III.5 Uniform Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></span></a>. Both the <math id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> deviations match almost perfectly with the linear baseline, and values are very small for both. These results show that network expressivity should be guided by the task at hand; when given the freedom to do so the model prefers to minimise unnecessary nonlinearity, with excessively flexible activations potentially degrading performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/final_symm_leakyrelu_main.svg" id="S3.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/142;" width="476" height="142"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span> (a) <math id="S3.F11.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and (b) relative <math id="S3.F11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation, both as a function of RG step. Both panels are plotted with the <math id="S3.F11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> gradient condition. All networks have symmetric weights.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/final_spline_main.svg" id="S3.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/287;" width="476" height="287"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span> (a), (b) <math id="S3.F12.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and (c), (d) relative <math id="S3.F12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation, both as a function of RG step. Legend labels refer to what parameters are trainable, e.g. ‘Spline’ corresponds to only having unfrozen spline parameters. Right hand panels are plotted without this ‘Spline’ condition.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that the fact that <math id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> values remain small while the <math id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation diverges at high RG iterations suggests that this behaviour is likely an insignificant numerical artefact. At higher iterations <math id="S3.SS5.SSS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> shrinks as the distribution becomes increasingly Gaussian, so very small cumulant values can manifest as large relative errors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span>Generalisation of GNNs</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">IV.1 </span>Tasks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we investigate the effect of architectural inductive biases introduced by GNNs on the generalisation of learned CLT transformations. We consider the same uniform task described in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="III.1 Tasks ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.1</span></span></a>, involving distributional shifts with higher-order cumulants over multiple composed RG steps. Generalisation is assessed via empirical cumulant deviation and KL divergence as before, averaging measurements over five different seeds to reduce estimation noise. When only cumulant information was available, the same Fourier inversion of the characteristic function was applied to reconstruct probability density functions, with the same frequency resolution and cutoff.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">GNNs are typically applied to large symmetric graphs where their built-in global permutation equivariance plays a significant role <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. However, rather than comparing performance amongst GNNs, the aim here is instead to better understand how their local aggregation mechanisms and architectural biases influence generalisation relative to MLPs. To this end, we apply GNNs to simple two-node directed graphs that mirror the two-to-one dimensionality reduction of <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a>, avoiding complexity from larger irregular graphs. Although these graphs lack node-level permutation symmetry, their use still enables comparison between the message-passing formalism and feedforward MLP architectures.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">IV.2 </span>Network Architecture and Training Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use shallow GNN architectures with either one or two layers, and low-dimensional hidden representations (1D or 2D), to minimise model capacity and reduce the risk of overfitting. This is appropriate given the small input graph size and scalar node features. Models were all implemented using <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PyTorch Geometric</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. To simplify analysis and support verification of the extended GNN cumulant propagation framework, we use linear SAGEConv layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> without any activation functions. Further hyperparameters and training details are included in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experimental Setup ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">IV.3 </span>Cumulant Propagation Validation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we evaluate the validity of the cumulant propagation framework under the independent node approximation that is encapsulated in <a href="#S2.E22" title="In II.3.2 Decomposing GNNs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.3 Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></span>)</a>. We consider the uniform task, and cumulants are propagated across networks layers within each step by iteratively applying <a href="#S2.E23" title="In II.3.2 Decomposing GNNs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.3 Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></span>)</a> and <a href="#S2.E24" title="In II.3.2 Decomposing GNNs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.3 Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></span>)</a> to the two-node directed graph we consider. For comparison, we use a linear MLP baseline with asymmetric weights and two affine layers, with cumulant propagation performed using <a href="#S2.E12" title="In II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></span>)</a> and <a href="#S2.E13" title="In II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></span>)</a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S4.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ IV.3 Cumulant Propagation Validation ‣ IV Generalisation of GNNs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></span></a>, the GNN is able to match the MLP baseline well to an extent, with initially small <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{prop}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>prop</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{prop}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math> values of order <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> and relative <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> deviations consistently within <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\%</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the framework is clearly limited. The <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation plots show much larger errors for the GNN than the MLP baseline, and the GNN fails to capture higher order cumulants, with relative deviations in <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> exceeding several hundred percent. These limitations are attributed to the crudeness of the independent node approximation. While sufficing for low order cumulants, it neglects important interactions induced by message passing. While node embeddings are initially independent from i.i.d. sampling, during training node representations become correlated both through the aggregation of neighbour information and the fact that weights are globally shared across nodes. These dependencies are essential for network learning dynamics and are not reflected in our framework, underlying its reduced performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F13" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/propagation_final.svg" id="S4.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/284;" width="476" height="284"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span><math id="S4.F13.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{prop}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>prop</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{prop}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math> and relative cumulant deviations for all of <math id="S4.F13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Propagation breaks down for higher order cumulants. While not shown, <math id="S4.F13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> values increase rapidly above 8 RG iterations; the plot instead focuses on the behaviour relative to the linear baseline for lower RG steps.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F14" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/generalisation_final.svg" id="S4.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/256;" width="429" height="256"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14: </span><math id="S4.F14.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math> and relative cumulant deviations for all of <math id="S4.F14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Generalisation is at most as good as the linear baseline.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">IV.4 </span>Uniform Task Generalisation Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The GNN architecture considered performs comparably to the MLP baseline (<a href="#S4.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ IV.3 Cumulant Propagation Validation ‣ IV Generalisation of GNNs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></span></a>). <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}(p_{\text{net}}\|p_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math> values are consistently very small, approximately of order <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the relative cumulant deviations match well. However, the GNN does show some limitations. Despite being initially of small magnitude, the GNN <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> values quickly spike above 8 RG steps, and the errors in relative GNN cumulant deviations are generally at least as large as those of the MLP.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although GNNs’ inductive biases can be very useful for arbitrarily complex structured data, these same biases can be counterproductive when the task lacks the structure that the GNN is designed to exploit. Here, the two-node directed graph has minimal symmetry or topological complexity, and does not present any permutation structure for the GNN’s permutation-equivariant operations to exploit. As a result, MLPs generalise slightly more effectively, with the GNN architecture possibly hindering learning by imposing inductive biases misaligned with the task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we note that both models superficially appear to consistently struggle predicting <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and fail to capture <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> well at high RG iterations. However, these are most likely numerical artefacts: for the symmetric uniform distribution <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, and both cumulants decay under the CLT, resulting in large relative errors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work primarily explores the role of symmetry and architectural expressivity in improving the generalisation of learned decimation RG transformations. Symmetry constraints on network parameters can often lead to improved parameter efficiency and better generalisation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>. However, we present a more nuanced perspective: networks must preserve the symmetries encoded in the RG transformation, but there is a complex interplay between symmetry constraints and network expressivity capacity, and symmetry constraints only aid generalisation when aligned with the representational demands of the task. This closely parallels the work of Ziyin et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, who argue that the principle of symmetry breaking and restoration in network parameters can unify theories of deep learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that in linear networks, symmetry has little effect due to limited expressivity. For networks using the quadratic activation, symmetric weights overconstrain the system and generalisation is poor. We show analytically that no solution exists for the small MLPs considered. ReLU activations offer stronger nonlinearity and perform even worse in the Gaussian task. In these cases, symmetry breaking is required for learning. Just as Goldstone modes emerge when continuous symmetries are broken in physical systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, asymmetric weights provide more representational degrees of freedom that are necessary for effective learning. These results are consistent with prior works that indicate how networks break symmetry to adapt to different tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In leaky ReLU networks with unconstrained weights we observe a sharp phase transition-like phenomenon. Models with weak nonlinearities show considerably worse generalisation than linear equivalents, but performance improves as nonlinearity increases. In contrast, symmetric weights clash with the internal asymmetry of the activation, resulting in frustrated learning dynamics. This competition between symmetry and expressivity echoes ideas from condensed matter physics, where frustrated systems cannot simultaneously minimise all local energy constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our work with both spline activations and GNNs reinforces this narrative. In both cases, the architectural inductive biases acted as an Occam’s razor: minimally but sufficiently expressive systems generalised best. Our results suggest that network expressivity and architectural bias should be matched to the structure of the task. In the spline case, the needlessly complex activation with fixed weights was more prone to overfitting and poor generalisation. When the weights were trainable, the model effectively reduced to a linear MLP with improved performance, reminiscent of the double descent phenomenon in machine learning. For GNNs, the built-in permutation equivariance combined with the single directed edge was misaligned with the structure of the CLT, limiting generalisation. Such findings are consistent with behaviour observed in many modern neural models, for instance in shortcut learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">From a physics perspective, our results aim to aid understanding of how renormalisation concepts and associated symmetries can be encoded within neural models to improve generalisation. We build on established connections between RG and hierarchical representations in deep networks to gain further insight into their capacity to act as models of coarse-graining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To interpret the internal information processing performed by GNNs, we also extend an existing framework that propagates cumulants through MLP models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> to this more complex architecture. We validate a simple first approximation that is able to consistently track the covariance over multiple RG steps to within <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\%</annotation></semantics></math>. However, it fails to capture crucial node correlations induced through training and could be improved upon with further theoretical modelling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">There are several limitations to this study. Firstly, the low dimensionality of the CLT transformation considered may limit the generality of our results. Additionally, this transformation is a particularly simple case and future work could extend similar analyses to more complex RG flows. Coarse-graining in physical systems like the Ising model typically involve structured dependencies that can be analysed probabilistically using Dyson’s hierarchical models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and offer a more realistic physics benchmark. GNNs are a natural architecture to capture such spatial dependencies. While prior work has evaluated the effect of equivariance on GNN generalisation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, the full potential of their inductive biases is not fully harnessed by the simplified tasks considered here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, the technique of analytically inverting cumulant propagation equations might not scale to more expressive or deeper models. Alternate approaches may be required to obtain similar analytical results to retain interpretability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span>Conclusions</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work demonstrates the existence of a critical balance between symmetry constraints and network expressivity that determines generalisation performance. Focusing on the central limit theorem as a controlled test case of a renormalisation flow, our analytical framework, which extends cumulant propagation theory from MLPs to GNNs, provides a clear lens through which to interpret how network models learn physically meaningful transformations. Our results indicate that symmetry constraints must be applied carefully, with the potential to harm generalisation by being overly restrictive. Equally, more flexible architectures can lead to reduced performance through overfitting or poor inductive alignment, reinforcing the importance of matching architectural bias with specific tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These observations suggest a broader design principle for neural network design in physics applications: effective architectures must leverage physical symmetries carefully, without constraining representational flexibility that is useful to capture essential features of underlying transformations. Besides investigation of more complex RG flows, future work could explore adaptively incorporating symmetry constraints during training (such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>), which potentially offers a more nuanced approach to encoding physical priors in network models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Central Limit Theorem Cumulant Recursion Relation</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start here from the one-step renormalisation relation between distributions, <a href="#S2.E3" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></span>)</a>:</p>
<table id="A1.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{n+1}(x)=\sqrt{2}\int dy\,p_{n}(\sqrt{2}x-y)\,p_{n}(y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{n+1}(x)=\sqrt{2}\int dy\,p_{n}(\sqrt{2}x-y)\,p_{n}(y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and consider the moment generating function</p>
<table id="A1.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{n+1}(s)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{sx}\,p_{n+1}(x)\,dx." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{n+1}(s)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{sx}\,p_{n+1}(x)\,dx.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Making the substitution <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=x\sqrt{2}-y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=x\sqrt{2}-y</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dx=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}du" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dx=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}du</annotation></semantics></math>, the convolution becomes</p>
<table id="A1.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{n+1}(s)=\int du\int\,dy\,e^{\frac{s(u+y)}{\sqrt{2}}}\,p_{n}(u)\,p_{n}(y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∫</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{n+1}(s)=\int du\int\,dy\,e^{\frac{s(u+y)}{\sqrt{2}}}\,p_{n}(u)\,p_{n}(y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">which is separable, yielding</p>
<table id="A1.E41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{n+1}(s)=\left[M_{n}\left(\frac{s}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\right]^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{n+1}(s)=\left[M_{n}\left(\frac{s}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\right]^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">The cumulant generating function is defined as <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n+1}(s)=\ln M_{n+1}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n+1}(s)=\ln M_{n+1}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> and the cumulants <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{n}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{n}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math> are the derivatives</p>
<table id="A1.E42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}^{(n)}=\left.\frac{d^{r}}{ds^{r}}K_{n}(s)\right|_{s=0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}^{(n)}=\left.\frac{d^{r}}{ds^{r}}K_{n}(s)\right|_{s=0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">By applying the chain rule to</p>
<table id="A1.E43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n+1}(s)=\ln\left[M_{n}\left(\frac{s}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^{2}\right]=2K_{n}\left(\frac{s}{\sqrt{2}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n+1}(s)=\ln\left[M_{n}\left(\frac{s}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^{2}\right]=2K_{n}\left(\frac{s}{\sqrt{2}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.7" class="ltx_p">and noting that the zeroth order cumulant is always zero from normalisation, we have the desired relation</p>
<table id="A1.E44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}^{(n+1)}=2^{1-r/2}\,\kappa_{r}^{(n)}\quad\text{for }r\geq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}^{(n+1)}=2^{1-r/2}\,\kappa_{r}^{(n)}\quad\text{for }r\geq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This result suggests the following three conservation laws are associated with the transformation <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>: normalisation, centreing, and variance. We have</p>
<table id="A1.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int p_{n+1}(x)\,dx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int p_{n+1}(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int p_{n}(x)\,dx," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int p_{n}(x)\,dx,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int xp_{n+1}(x)\,dx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int xp_{n+1}(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int xp_{n}(x)\,dx," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int xp_{n}(x)\,dx,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int x^{2}p_{n+1}(x)\,dx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int x^{2}p_{n+1}(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int x^{2}p_{n}(x)\,dx." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int x^{2}p_{n}(x)\,dx.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">A centred distribution therefore preserves its variance with all higher order cumulants vanishing, which is the essence of the CLT.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix contains more detail on the cumulant propagation framework outlined in <a href="#S2.SS2.SSS2" title="II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.2.2</span></a> for MLPs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first consider cumulant propagation across the nonlinear activation function <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> by writing</p>
<table id="A2.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{y^{l}}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{W}_{y^{l}}(j)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}y^{l})\rangle_{y^{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}y^{l})\rangle_{y^{l}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}\phi(z^{l}))\rangle_{z^{l}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ln\langle\exp(j^{\top}\phi(z^{l}))\rangle_{z^{l}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">As noted in the main text, this generating function cannot be evaluated exactly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> in general. Perturbative techniques that exist to overcome this difficulty proceed by replacing <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(z^{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(z^{l})</annotation></semantics></math> with its Taylor expansion <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{m}\frac{\phi^{(m)}\big|_{z^{l}=0}}{m!}(z^{l})^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mfrac><msub><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{m}\frac{\phi^{(m)}\big|_{z^{l}=0}}{m!}(z^{l})^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and treat the nonlinear terms (<math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>) as being small <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. By expanding the activation function in this way it is possible to determine analytic expressions for the cumulants <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{y^{l}}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{y^{l}}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math> of the postactivations <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of those of the preactivations <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for arbitrary activation functions. More information is contained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>; for example, how to construct postactivation cumulants as a series of Feynman diagrams. Cumulants <math id="A2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{y^{l}}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{y^{l}}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="A2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> are typically functions of multiple cumulants of the preactivations <math id="A2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> of different orders <math id="A2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{y^{l}}^{(n)}=f_{n}(\{G_{z^{l}}^{(m)}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{y^{l}}^{(n)}=f_{n}(\{G_{z^{l}}^{(m)}\})</annotation></semantics></math>, representing the information transfer between layers. This behaviour is distinct from the affine transformations which do not mix cumulants. Such a perturbative approach assumes a differentiable activation function, but it is possible to adapt the method for nondifferentiable functions such as ReLU by using a Gram-Charlier expansion of the probability distribution <math id="A2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z^{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z^{l})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our analytical results for symmetrically-constrained weights in MLPs hinge upon this propagation framework for a quadratic nonlinearity <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(z)=z+\alpha z^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(z)=z+\alpha z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This particular activation has relatively simple expressions for the interaction functions <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1,2}(G_{z^{l}}^{(m)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1,2}(G_{z^{l}}^{(m)})</annotation></semantics></math> of the first two cumulants <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, the mean and variance. These are</p>
<table id="A2.E49" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="A2.EGx17" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E49.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E49.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu_{y^{l},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu_{y^{l},i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E49.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mu_{z^{l},i}+\alpha(\mu_{z^{l},i})^{2}+\alpha\,\Sigma_{z^{l},ii}\;," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mu_{z^{l},i}+\alpha(\mu_{z^{l},i})^{2}+\alpha\,\Sigma_{z^{l},ii}\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E49.x1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E49.x1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Sigma_{y^{l},ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Sigma_{y^{l},ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E49.x1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Sigma_{z^{l},ij}+2\alpha\,\Sigma_{z^{l},ij}(\mu_{z^{l},i}+\mu_{z^{l},j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Sigma_{z^{l},ij}+2\alpha\,\Sigma_{z^{l},ij}(\mu_{z^{l},i}+\mu_{z^{l},j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E49.x2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E49.x2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+2\alpha^{2}(\Sigma_{z^{l},ij})^{2}+4\alpha^{2}\mu_{z^{l},i}\,\Sigma_{z^{l},ij}\,\mu_{z^{l},j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+2\alpha^{2}(\Sigma_{z^{l},ij})^{2}+4\alpha^{2}\mu_{z^{l},i}\,\Sigma_{z^{l},ij}\,\mu_{z^{l},j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E49.x3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E49.x3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\alpha(1+2\alpha\,\mu_{z^{l},i})\,G_{z^{l},(i,i,j)}^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\alpha(1+2\alpha\,\mu_{z^{l},i})\,G_{z^{l},(i,i,j)}^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E49.x4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E49.x4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\alpha(1+2\alpha\,\mu_{z^{l},j})\,G_{z^{l},(j,j,i)}^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\alpha(1+2\alpha\,\mu_{z^{l},j})\,G_{z^{l},(j,j,i)}^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E49.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E49.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\alpha^{2}\,G_{z^{l},(i,i,j,j)}^{(4)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\alpha^{2}\,G_{z^{l},(i,i,j,j)}^{(4)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By composing the transformations for cumulants across affine layers, and the relevant interaction functions <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{n}(\{G^{(m)}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{n}(\{G^{(m)}\})</annotation></semantics></math> for the choice of activation function, for each order <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> we can write</p>
<table id="A2.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{y}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{y}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=W^{L+1}\left(f_{n}\left(\cdots\{G_{x}^{(m)}\}_{m}\cdots\right)\right)+b^{L+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=W^{L+1}\left(f_{n}\left(\cdots\{G_{x}^{(m)}\}_{m}\cdots\right)\right)+b^{L+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=:g_{n}\left(\{G_{x}^{(m)}\}_{m};\theta,\phi\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=:g_{n}\left(\{G_{x}^{(m)}\}_{m};\theta,\phi\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">As the nonlinearity mixes orders of cumulants, each cumulant of the network output will in general depend on the full set of input cumulants. The <span id="A2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">statistical model</span> of the network is then defined as the mapping</p>
<table id="A2.E51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{stat}}:\left(\left\{G_{x}^{(m)}\right\}_{m},\theta,\phi\right)\mapsto p(y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>stat</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{stat}}:\left(\left\{G_{x}^{(m)}\right\}_{m},\theta,\phi\right)\mapsto p(y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is characterised by the propagated cumulants. It is important to note that there is a one-to-one correspondence between the statistical model and the network model <math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\;:\;(x,\theta)\mapsto y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\;:\;(x,\theta)\mapsto y</annotation></semantics></math>, as they both share the same parameters <math id="A2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{W^{l},b^{l}\}_{l=1,\dots,L+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{W^{l},b^{l}\}_{l=1,\dots,L+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond empirical comparisons with the corresponding network model, the statistical model can be used to determine the importance of data correlations <math id="A2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{G_{x}^{(n)}\right\}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{G_{x}^{(n)}\right\}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to a given task. For example, in classification tasks the network learns to minimise the expectation of a loss function <math id="A2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(y,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(y,t)</annotation></semantics></math> between the network outputs <math id="A2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and class labels <math id="A2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. It can be shown that the common choice of
mean-squared error loss, <math id="A2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{MSE}(y,t)=\|y-t\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{MSE}(y,t)=\|y-t\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, has an expected value that depends only on the mean <math id="A2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{t}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{t}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and variance <math id="A2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{t}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{t}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> of outputs of each class <math id="A2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> as</p>
<table id="A2.E52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{MSE}}(\{\mu_{y}^{t},\Sigma_{y}^{t};t\})=\sum_{t}p(t)\left(\mathrm{tr}\,\Sigma_{y}^{t}+\|\mu_{y}^{t}-t\|^{2}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>MSE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{MSE}}(\{\mu_{y}^{t},\Sigma_{y}^{t};t\})=\sum_{t}p(t)\left(\mathrm{tr}\,\Sigma_{y}^{t}+\|\mu_{y}^{t}-t\|^{2}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the distribution associated with a particular set of labels <math id="A2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. For this loss function, network training and information processing is therefore directly related to understanding how the first- and second-order cumulants of the output arise from the propagation of input data correlation functions. Fischer et al. propose and empirically verify a Gaussian approximation of the statistical model for sufficiently wide networks that use this loss function, in which the hidden layers only pass on the Gaussian part of the statistics <math id="A2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{z^{l}}^{(1)},G_{z^{l}}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{z^{l}}^{(1)},G_{z^{l}}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. While interesting, this is not relevant to the present discussion and we refer readers to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> for more information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Single Linear Layer</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Networks with a single linear layer and no biases have only two parameters, contained within the weights matrix <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=(w_{1},w_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=(w_{1},w_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times 2}</annotation></semantics></math>. By comparison with <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a> we can immediately identify <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}=w_{1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}=w_{1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Besides direct recognition, this result could be obtained by using the cumulant propagation framework to solve for network parameters. However, here we consider an alternative least-squares approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first write the network mapping</p>
<table id="A3.E53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\begin{pmatrix}w_{1}&amp;w_{2}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\xi_{1}\\
\xi_{2}\end{pmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\begin{pmatrix}w_{1}&amp;w_{2}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\xi_{1}\\
\xi_{2}\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">in keeping with the notation of <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a>, and consider some loss function between network output <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and the desired mapping <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\xi_{1}+\xi_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\xi_{1}+\xi_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="A3.E54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\langle\Big[w_{1}\xi_{1}+w_{2}\xi_{2}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\xi_{1}+\xi_{2})\Big]^{2}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\langle\Big[w_{1}\xi_{1}+w_{2}\xi_{2}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\xi_{1}+\xi_{2})\Big]^{2}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Assuming the inputs are both centred i.i.d. variables of variance <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\xi_{1}\xi_{2}\rangle=\langle\xi_{1}\rangle\langle\xi_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\xi_{1}\xi_{2}\rangle=\langle\xi_{1}\rangle\langle\xi_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, and defining <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}=w_{1}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}=w_{1}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2}=w_{2}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2}=w_{2}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="A3.E55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=(\alpha_{1}^{2}+\alpha_{2}^{2})\sigma^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=(\alpha_{1}^{2}+\alpha_{2}^{2})\sigma^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Minimisation with respect to <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> directly gives <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}=w_{2}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}=w_{2}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F15" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/gaussian_datasets_WLETTER.svg" id="A3.F15.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/105;" width="476" height="105"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 15: </span>(a) Mean deviation between empirical ground truth and predicted <math id="A3.F15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> values (using <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a>) as a function of sample size <math id="A3.F15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> in the Gaussian task. The theoretical standard deviation <math id="A3.F15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{24/N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{24/N}</annotation></semantics></math> of the sample <math id="A3.F15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> estimator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> is overlaid for reference.
(b), (c) The mean <math id="A3.F15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.F15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norms of off-diagonal elements in input tensor cumulants against <math id="A3.F15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A3.F15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> was used to balance computational tractability with small <math id="A3.F15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> error and off-diagonal magnitude.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F16" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.16591v1/uniform_datasets_WLETTER.png" id="A3.F16.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/104;" width="476" height="104" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 16: </span>(a) Mean deviation between empirical ground truth and predicted cumulant values (using <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a>) as a function of sample size <math id="A3.F16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> in the uniform task, for <math id="A3.F16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2},\kappa_{3},\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2},\kappa_{3},\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. (b), (c) The mean <math id="A3.F16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.F16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norms of off-diagonal elements in input tensor cumulants are plotted against <math id="A3.F16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. As in the Gaussian task, datasets of size <math id="A3.F16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> were chosen to balance computational tractability with small <math id="A3.F16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> error and off-diagonal magnitude.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F17" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.16591v1/final_asymm_leakyrelu_appendix.png" id="A3.F17.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/291;" width="476" height="291" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 17: </span> Relative cumulant deviations as a function of RG steps. (a), (c) are plotted for <math id="A3.F17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (b), (d) for <math id="A3.F17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Top panels are plotted with <math id="A3.F17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> gradient, bottom panels are without. All networks have unconstrained weights, and use the same legend.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F18" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.16591v1/final_symm_leakyrelu_appendix.svg" id="A3.F18.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/148;" width="476" height="148"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 18: </span> Relative cumulant deviations as a function of RG steps. (a), (b) are plotted for <math id="A3.F18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.F18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. All networks have symmetric weights, and use the same legend.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With biases, the network mapping becomes</p>
<table id="A3.E56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\begin{pmatrix}w_{1}&amp;w_{2}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\xi_{1}\\
\xi_{2}\end{pmatrix}+b" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\begin{pmatrix}w_{1}&amp;w_{2}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\xi_{1}\\
\xi_{2}\end{pmatrix}+b</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and we consider the loss function</p>
<table id="A3.E57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\langle\Big[w_{1}\xi_{1}+w_{2}\xi_{2}+b-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\xi_{1}+\xi_{2})\Big]^{2}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\langle\Big[w_{1}\xi_{1}+w_{2}\xi_{2}+b-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\xi_{1}+\xi_{2})\Big]^{2}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(57)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Again assuming i.i.d. input variables but now allowing for non-zero mean <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=\langle\xi\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\langle\xi\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=\langle\xi^{2}\rangle-\langle\xi\rangle^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=\langle\xi^{2}\rangle-\langle\xi\rangle^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Making the same substitutions for <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the loss becomes</p>
<table id="A3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=b^{2}+2b(\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2})\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=b^{2}+2b(\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2})\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+(\alpha_{1}^{2}+\alpha_{2}^{2})\sigma^{2}+(\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2})^{2}\mu^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+(\alpha_{1}^{2}+\alpha_{2}^{2})\sigma^{2}+(\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2})^{2}\mu^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(58)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Minimising with respect to <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> yields that <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}=\alpha_{2}=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}=\alpha_{2}=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=-2\alpha\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=-2\alpha\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, from which we deduce that</p>
<table id="A3.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{1}=w_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{1}=w_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(59)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Two Linear Layers</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we derive <a href="#S2.E33" title="In II.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></span>)</a>, using the approach outlined in <a href="#S2.SS4" title="II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.4</span></span></a>. Consider the symmetrically constrained weights</p>
<table id="A3.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{0}&amp;w_{1}\\
w_{1}&amp;w_{0}\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{0}&amp;w_{1}\\
w_{1}&amp;w_{0}\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(60)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E61"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E61.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{2}&amp;w_{2}\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{2}&amp;w_{2}\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(61)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the input cumulants</p>
<table id="A3.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E62"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{x,\;i}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{x,\;i}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E62.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}\kappa_{1}\\
\kappa_{1}\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}\kappa_{1}\\
\kappa_{1}\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(62)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E63"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{x,\;ij}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{x,\;ij}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E63.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}\kappa_{2}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\kappa_{2}\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}\kappa_{2}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\kappa_{2}\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(63)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E64"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{x,\;ijk}^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{x,\;ijk}^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E64.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\kappa_{3},&amp;\text{if }i=j=k\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\kappa_{3},&amp;\text{if }i=j=k\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(64)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which are diagonal as the inputs are i.i.d. variables. Transforming these according to <a href="#S2.E12" title="In II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></span>)</a> and <a href="#S2.E13" title="In II.2.2 Decomposing MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ II.2 Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></span>)</a>, we find the cumulants of the outputs <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> to be</p>
<table id="A3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E65"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{y}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{y}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E65.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2\kappa_{1}w_{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2\kappa_{1}w_{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(65)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E66"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{y}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{y}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E66.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2\kappa_{2}w_{2}^{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2\kappa_{2}w_{2}^{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(66)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E67"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{y}^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{y}^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E67.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2\kappa_{3}w_{2}^{3}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{3}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2\kappa_{3}w_{2}^{3}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(67)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a> we can set <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{y}^{(1)}=\sqrt{2}G_{x}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{y}^{(1)}=\sqrt{2}G_{x}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{y}^{(2)}=G_{x}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{y}^{(2)}=G_{x}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{y}^{(3)}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}G_{x}^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{y}^{(3)}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}G_{x}^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Solving this set of simultaneous equations then yields the desired result <a href="#S2.E33" title="In II.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></span>)</a>:</p>
<table id="A3.E68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}=\frac{1}{w_{2}\sqrt{2}}-w_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}=\frac{1}{w_{2}\sqrt{2}}-w_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(68)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To introduce biases, define <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=(b_{1},b_{1})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=(b_{1},b_{1})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> for the first layer and <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for the second. Two additional parameters necessitates transforming cumulants up to fifth order. With <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}^{(4)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}^{(4)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}^{(5)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}^{(5)}</annotation></semantics></math> only non-zero when their indices are all the same as in <a href="#A3.E64" title="In C.2 Two Linear Layers ‣ Appendix C CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">64</span></span>)</a>, we can perform analogous tensor contractions and use <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a> to find</p>
<table id="A3.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E69"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}\Big[(w_{0}+w_{1})\kappa_{1}+b_{1}\Big]+b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}\Big[(w_{0}+w_{1})\kappa_{1}+b_{1}\Big]+b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E69.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\kappa_{1}\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\kappa_{1}\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(69)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E70"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}^{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}^{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E70.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(70)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E71"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}^{3}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}^{3}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E71.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(71)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E72"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}^{4}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}^{4}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E72.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(72)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E73"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}^{5}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}^{5}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{5}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E73.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(73)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Solving this set of equations yields <a href="#S2.E34" title="In II.4.1 Linear Networks ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></span>)</a></p>
<table id="A3.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E74"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E74.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{w_{2}\sqrt{2}}-w_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{w_{2}\sqrt{2}}-w_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(74)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E75"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E75.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-2w_{2}b_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-2w_{2}b_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(75)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">All tensor contractions were checked using the SymPy library on Python (see <a href="https://docs.sympy.org/latest/index.html" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">here</a> for more information).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Quadratic Nonlinearity</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we show how the inconsistency in <a href="#S2.E36" title="In II.4.2 Quadratic Nonlinearity ‣ II.4 CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></span>)</a> arises from cumulant propagation in networks with two linear layers as above, with the same weights, no biases, and an additional quadratic nonlinearity <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(z)=z+\alpha z^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(z)=z+\alpha z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Proceeding layerwise, after the first affine transformation the first two cumulants are</p>
<table id="A3.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E76"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{z^{1}}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{z^{1}}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E76.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(w_{0}+w_{1})\kappa_{1}\begin{pmatrix}1\\
1\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(w_{0}+w_{1})\kappa_{1}\begin{pmatrix}1\\
1\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(76)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E77"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{z^{1}}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{z^{1}}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E77.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\kappa_{2}\begin{pmatrix}w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2}&amp;2w_{0}w_{1}\\
2w_{0}w_{1}&amp;w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2}\end{pmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\kappa_{2}\begin{pmatrix}w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2}&amp;2w_{0}w_{1}\\
2w_{0}w_{1}&amp;w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2}\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(77)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Applying the interaction function in <a href="#A2.E49.1" title="In 49 ‣ Appendix B MLPs as Mappings of Correlation Functions ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49a</span></span>)</a>, after the nonlinear activation the first cumulant becomes</p>
<table id="A3.E78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{y^{1},\;i}^{(1)}=(w_{0}+w_{1})\kappa_{1}+\alpha\kappa_{1}^{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}+\alpha\kappa_{2}(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{y^{1},\;i}^{(1)}=(w_{0}+w_{1})\kappa_{1}+\alpha\kappa_{1}^{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}+\alpha\kappa_{2}(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(78)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which is the same for both elements <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{y^{1},\;1}^{(1)},G_{y^{1},\;2}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{y^{1},\;1}^{(1)},G_{y^{1},\;2}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. After the second affine transformation, comparison with the expected cumulant evolution in <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II.1 CLT as an RG Transformation ‣ II Theory ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Eq. (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span>)</a> yields</p>
<table id="A3.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\kappa_{1}\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\kappa_{1}\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=2w_{2}\Big[(w_{0}+w_{1})\kappa_{1}+\alpha\kappa_{1}^{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2w_{2}\Big[(w_{0}+w_{1})\kappa_{1}+\alpha\kappa_{1}^{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E79"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E79.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\alpha\kappa_{2}(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})\Big]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\alpha\kappa_{2}(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})\Big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(79)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Successful learning of the transformation means that this relationship should hold for any values of <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, comparing coefficients leads to the equations</p>
<table id="A3.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}+w_{1})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E80"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E80.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(80)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.5" class="ltx_p">and it is clear from the latter two that this set has a contradiction.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Weights could alternately be constrained as</p>
<table id="A3.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E81"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E81.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{0}&amp;w_{0}\\
w_{1}&amp;w_{1}\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{0}&amp;w_{0}\\
w_{1}&amp;w_{1}\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(81)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E82"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E82.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{2}&amp;w_{2}\end{pmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}w_{2}&amp;w_{2}\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(82)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">as these satisfy the constraint <math id="A3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{2}W^{1}P=W^{2}W^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{2}W^{1}P=W^{2}W^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> which preserves the required <math id="A3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> symmetry. This ’columns-same’ approach is less expressive as it collapses the representation to a one-dimensional subspace of parameters, whereas weights that are symmetric across the diagonal preserve the two-dimensionality of the hidden representation space. Nonetheless, in this case we draw the same conclusions. Following the exact same steps leads to the equations</p>
<table id="A3.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}(w_{0}+w_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 4w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 4w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E83"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2w_{2}\alpha(w_{0}^{2}+w_{1}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E83.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(83)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">which have no real solution.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix contains key hyperparameters and training details used to implement network models as in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III.2 Network Architecture and Training Setup ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.2</span></span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV.2 Network Architecture and Training Setup ‣ IV Generalisation of GNNs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV.2</span></span></a>. We also include empirical results justifying our choice of data set size <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> for both the Gaussian and uniform task in <a href="#A3.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ C.1 Single Linear Layer ‣ Appendix C CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></span></a> and <a href="#A3.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ C.1 Single Linear Layer ‣ Appendix C CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>MLPs</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before training, network parameters were all randomly initialised from i.i.d. centred Gaussians <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{ij}^{\text{i.i.d.}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{w}^{2}/N_{l-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mtext>i.i.d.</mtext></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{ij}^{\text{i.i.d.}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{w}^{2}/N_{l-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}^{\text{i.i.d.}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{b}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>i.i.d.</mtext></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}^{\text{i.i.d.}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{b}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. The variance scaling <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{w}^{2}/N_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{w}^{2}/N_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is such that the covariance of <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the layer width, as inspired by both Glorot &amp; Bengio and He et al <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Variances <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{w}^{2}=\sigma_{b}^{2}=0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{w}^{2}=\sigma_{b}^{2}=0.75</annotation></semantics></math> were used to initialise network parameters <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Networks were all trained using the empirical MSE loss per data batch <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x^{(b)},t^{(b)})\}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x^{(b)},t^{(b)})\}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A4.E84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{emp},\text{MSE}}(\theta)=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\|g(x^{(b)};\theta)-t^{(b)}\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mtext>emp</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>MSE</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{emp},\text{MSE}}(\theta)=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\|g(x^{(b)};\theta)-t^{(b)}\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(84)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Training was limited to two epochs to investigate generalisation before models overfitted to specific tasks. Batch size <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> was set to 64. <span id="A4.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ADAM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> was used for optimisation with learning rate <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, momenta <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>, and weight decay <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The choice of optimiser doesn’t affect any previous theoretical results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>GNNs</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Parameters <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> were initialised from i.i.d. Gaussians with <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{w}^{2}=\sigma_{b}^{2}=0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{w}^{2}=\sigma_{b}^{2}=0.75</annotation></semantics></math> as for MLPs. Networks were trained using the empirical MSE loss per data batch, with batch size <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Adam</span> was used for optimisation with the same hyperparameters as above. While weights were not actively constrained to be symmetric, we added a soft symmetry regularisation term to the loss to improve training stability. A weight norm penalty was also used to prevent weights from collapsing to vanishingly small values. Due to the directed nature of input graphs, networks were trained solely on the embeddings of target nodes. As for MLPs, all data sets were of size <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> and shuffled duplicates of network outputs were used as inputs for successive RG steps to ensure i.i.d. conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>MLP Uniform Task: Leaky ReLU Results</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ III.5.2 Leaky ReLU with Asymmetric Weights ‣ III.5 Uniform Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></span></a> and <a href="#S3.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ III.5.4 Spline Activation with Asymmetric Weights ‣ III.5 Uniform Task Results ‣ III Generalisation of MLPs ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></span></a> in the main text illustrate the key findings of our experiments using the leaky ReLU activation. For completeness, we include here the relevant cumulant deviation graphs for <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in both cases. <a href="#A3.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ C.1 Single Linear Layer ‣ Appendix C CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></span></a> contains results for asymmetric weights, while <a href="#A3.F18" title="Figure 18 ‣ C.1 Single Linear Layer ‣ Appendix C CLT: Analytical Solutions for Constrained MLP Weights ‣ Symmetry and Generalisation in Neural Approximations of Renormalisation Transformations" class="ltx_ref">Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></span></a> contains those for symmetrically-constrained weights.</p>
</div>
</section>
